///|
/// The Unicode general categories, as ranges of code points.
///
/// RFC 9485 §3 requires full support for the Unicode property set — `\p{Lu}`
/// and its siblings — and a table is the only way to answer them. This one is
/// generated from the Unicode Character Database by
/// `python scripts/gen_fixtures.py categories`, one range per run of
/// consecutive code points in the same category; a code point that falls
/// outside every range is `Cn`, unassigned.
///
/// The third number is the category, in the order the table in `regexp.mbt`
/// names them: `Lu`, `Ll`, `Lt`, `Lm`, `Lo`, `Mn`, `Mc`, `Me`, `Nd`, `Nl`,
/// `No`, `Pc`, `Pd`, `Ps`, `Pe`, `Pi`, `Pf`, `Po`, `Sm`, `Sc`, `Sk`, `So`,
/// `Zs`, `Zl`, `Zp`, `Cc`, `Cf`, `Co`, `Cs`, `Cn`. `Cs` has no ranges: a
/// surrogate is half a character, and a MoonBit string never holds one.
let ranges : Array[(Int32, Int32, Int32)] = [
